WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.120
How does first come, first

2
00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.600
served scheduling work? Select the

3
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.220
first ready process and let

4
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.640
it keep the processor until

5
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.720
it blocks or finishes. This

6
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.300
is nonpreemptive scheduling, so a

7
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.960
later short job cannot simply

8
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.240
take the processor away.

9
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.020
Use the lecture example: P

10
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.320
one needs twenty-four processor units,

11
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:21.660
and P two and P

12
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.800
three each need three. All

13
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.560
arrive at zero, with the

14
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.060
stated queue order P one,

15
00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.400
P two, P three. Ignore

16
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.860
input-output and context-switch costs for

17
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:32.360
this trace.

18
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.020
P one begins immediately and

19
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.860
runs from zero to twenty-four.

20
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.320
During that long interval, P

21
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:40.780
two and P three remain

22
00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.620
ready but cannot preempt it.

23
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.060
Watch their remaining execution stay

24
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.860
unchanged while their waiting time

25
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:47.300
grows.

26
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.580
At twenty-four, P one completes

27
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.160
and P two runs for

28
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.100
its three units, finishing at

29
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.580
twenty-seven. P three then runs

30
00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.340
from twenty-seven to thirty. The

31
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.080
schedule follows the original queue

32
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.220
order, not a fresh comparison

33
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:04.200
of burst lengths.

34
00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.280
For this single-burst example, waiting

35
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.720
equals completion minus arrival minus

36
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.740
burst. P one waits zero,

37
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.180
P two waits twenty-four, and

38
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.520
P three waits twenty-seven. Their

39
00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.600
total waiting is fifty-one, divided

40
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.900
by three processes, giving an

41
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:21.840
average of seventeen.

42
00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.600
Now replay the same three

43
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.840
burst lengths with the short

44
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.980
jobs first: P two, P

45
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.740
three, then P one. The

46
00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:32.260
short jobs run from zero

47
00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.420
to three and three to

48
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.260
six; the long job finishes

49
00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.500
at thirty. We changed order,

50
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.540
not the total processor work.

51
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.440
In that reordered trace, the

52
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:42.980
original long job waits six,

53
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:44.860
the first short job waits

54
00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:46.420
zero, and the second waits

55
00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.480
three. Six plus zero plus

56
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.480
three, divided by three, gives

57
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:51.800
an average waiting time of

58
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:53.520
three. The last completion is

59
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.620
still thirty, but individual delays

60
00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:56.420
differ greatly.

61
00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.040
This illustrates the convoy effect:

62
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.280
short ready jobs wait behind

63
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.260
an earlier long job. First

64
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.380
come, first served is simple

65
00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.040
and respects its queue rule,

66
00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.720
but it does not minimize

67
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.060
every response or waiting objective.

68
00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:13.480
A scheduling policy has consequences

69
00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.180
even when no work is

70
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:15.460
lost.

71
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.260
With different arrival times, do

72
00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:19.860
not schedule a process before

73
00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.060
it exists in the ready

74
00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.980
set. If no process is

75
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.920
ready, show idle time rather

76
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:26.340
than moving a future arrival

77
00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.660
backwards. Simultaneous arrivals also need

78
00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:30.900
a stated queue tie rule.

79
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:32.740
Things to take note of.

80
00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.300
First come, first served is

81
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.980
nonpreemptive, its outcome depends on

82
00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.160
queue order, and completion time

83
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.460
is not waiting time. Reconstruct

84
00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.260
the Gantt chart first, then

85
00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.460
calculate per-process delays and their

86
00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:46.840
average.
